IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ha-types`
IMPORT `ha-pay`
IMPORT `ha-annual`

-- Holidays Act 2003 — GOALS 4 AND 5: SICK, BEREAVEMENT AND FAMILY VIOLENCE
-- LEAVE.
--
--   "Is the employee entitled to the leave; how many days of it; how much
--    must the employer pay, and when?"
--
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/act_public_2003_129.txt — ss 63-72,
-- 72A-72J; Schedule 1AA cls 4, 5.

§ `Goals 4 and 5 — sick, bereavement and family violence leave`

§§ `When the entitlement arises (ss 63, 72D)`

-- s 63(1) "An employee is entitled to sick leave and bereavement leave ...
-- (a) after the employee has completed 6 months' current continuous
-- employment with the employer; or (b) if ... subsection (1)(a) does not
-- apply, the employee has, over a period of 6 months, worked for the
-- employer for— (i) at least an average of 10 hours a week during that
-- period; and (ii) no less than 1 hour in every week ... or no less than 40
-- hours in every month". s 72D(1) is in the same words for family violence
-- leave. (2)(b) "as long as the circumstances referred to in subparagraphs
-- (i) and (ii) of that subsection continue to apply".
@ref ss 63(1), 72D(1)
GIVEN s IS A Service
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the entitlement first arises under s 63(1)(a) for` s MEANS
    `add months` (s's `current continuous employment began on`) 6

@ref ss 63(1)-(2), 72D(1)-(2)
GIVEN s IS A Service, d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `entitled under s 63(1) or 72D(1)` s `on` d IF
        d AT LEAST `the day the entitlement first arises under s 63(1)(a) for` s
    OR  CONSIDER s's `the hours test was first met over 6 months ending on`
        WHEN JUST e THEN d GREATER THAN e AND s's `the hours test still applies`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE

-- s 63(2) "(a) ... for— (i) the 12-month period of continuous employment
-- beginning at the end of the 6-month period ...; and (ii) each subsequent
-- 12 months". The first day of the 12-month period that contains d.
@ref ss 63(2), 72D(2)
GIVEN first IS A DATE, k IS A NUMBER, d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the entitlement period from` first `, period` k `, containing` d MEANS
    IF   `add years` first (k PLUS 1) GREATER THAN d
    THEN `add years` first k
    ELSE `the entitlement period from` first `, period` (k PLUS 1) `, containing` d

@ref ss 63(2), 72D(2)
GIVEN d IS A DATE, s IS A Service
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day of the 12-month entitlement period containing` d `for` s MEANS
    `the entitlement period from` first `, period` 0 `, containing` d
    WHERE
        first MEANS
            CONSIDER s's `the hours test was first met over 6 months ending on`
            WHEN JUST e THEN
                IF   `the day` 1 `days after` e LESS THAN `the day the entitlement first arises under s 63(1)(a) for` s
                THEN `the day` 1 `days after` e
                ELSE `the day the entitlement first arises under s 63(1)(a) for` s
            WHEN NOTHING THEN `the day the entitlement first arises under s 63(1)(a) for` s

§§ `Sick leave (ss 65-68)`

-- The day the Holidays (Increasing Sick Leave) Amendment Act 2021 came into
-- force (Schedule 1AA cl 5(4) "commencement date"; the date is that Act's,
-- not this text's).
`the 2021 commencement date` MEANS Date 24 7 2021

-- s 65(2) "An employee is entitled to 10 days' sick leave for each of the
-- 12-month periods specified in section 63(2)." Schedule 1AA cl 5(2): an
-- existing employee has 10 days "when [they] next become entitled to sick
-- leave for a 12-month period" on and from the commencement date. Periods
-- that began earlier fall under s 65(2) as it stood before 2021, which the
-- text does not carry.
@ref s 65(2); Schedule 1AA cl 5
GIVEN `period began` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the sick leave for the 12-month period beginning` `period began` MEANS
    IF   `period began` AT LEAST `the 2021 commencement date`
    THEN 10
    ELSE `a 12-month period that began before 24 July 2021 falls under s 65(2) as it stood before the 2021 amendment, which this encoding does not carry`

`a 12-month period that began before 24 July 2021 falls under s 65(2) as it stood before the 2021 amendment, which this encoding does not carry` MEANS
    REFUSE "a 12-month period that began before 24 July 2021 falls under s 65(2) as it stood before the 2021 amendment, which this encoding does not carry"

-- s 66 "(1) An employee may carry over, to any subsequent 12-month period
-- ..., any sick leave that has not been taken by the end of the period ...
-- (2) ... an employee may carry over up to 10 days' sick leave to a maximum
-- of 20 days' current entitlement in any year." FORK F11: the new period's
-- 10 days plus the unused balance carried over, up to 10 of it; the total
-- cannot exceed 20.
@ref s 66; Schedule 1AA cl 5(3)
@export The sick leave available at the start of a 12-month period under ss 65 and 66 of the Holidays Act 2003 (NZ): the period's 10 days plus up to 10 unused days carried over, to a maximum of 20; for periods beginning on or after 24 July 2021. DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN `period began` IS A DATE  @desc The first day of the new 12-month period
      unused IS A NUMBER  @desc The sick leave not taken by the end of the previous period
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the sick leave available for the period beginning` `period began` `, unused` unused MEANS
    min 20 (`the sick leave for the 12-month period beginning` `period began` PLUS min 10 (max 0 unused))

-- s 65(1) "An employee may take sick leave if— (a) the employee is sick or
-- injured; or (b) the employee's spouse or partner is sick or injured; or (c)
-- a person who depends on the employee for care is sick or injured."
@ref s 65(1)
GIVEN w IS A `Whose sickness`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a ground for sick leave` w IF
    NOT w EQUALS `someone else`

-- s 68 "(1) An employer may require an employee to produce proof of
-- sickness or injury ... if the sickness or injury ... is for a period of 3
-- or more consecutive calendar days, whether or not the days would otherwise
-- be working days ... (1A) Despite subsection (1), the employer may require
-- proof ... within 3 consecutive calendar days if the employer— (a) informs
-- the employee as early as possible that the proof is required; and (b)
-- agrees to meet the employee's reasonable expenses in obtaining the proof.
-- (2) Subsection (1) does not prevent an employer and employee from agreeing
-- that the employee will produce proof ... for sick leave provided ... in
-- addition to the entitlement set out in section 65."
@ref s 68
GIVEN p IS A `Proof request`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employer may require proof of sickness or injury` p IF
        p's `consecutive calendar days of sickness or injury` AT LEAST 3
    OR  (    p's `the employer informed the employee as early as possible`
         AND p's `the employer agrees to meet reasonable expenses`)
    OR  p's `the leave is beyond the s 65 entitlement and proof was agreed`

§§ `Bereavement leave (ss 69, 70)`

-- s 70(1) "An employer must allow an employee to take— (a) 3 days'
-- bereavement leave for each type of bereavement described in section
-- 69(2)(a), (c), and (d); and (b) 1 day's bereavement leave for a
-- bereavement described in section 69(2)(b)." s 69(2)(b) only "if the
-- employer accepts ... that the employee has suffered a bereavement".
@ref ss 69(2), 70(1)
GIVEN b IS A Bereavement
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the bereavement leave for` b MEANS
    CONSIDER b
    WHEN `(a) the death of a spouse or partner, parent, child, brother or sister, grandparent, grandchild, or spouse's or partner's parent` THEN 3
    WHEN `(b) the death of another person, if the employer accepts the employee has suffered a bereavement` THEN 1
    WHEN `(b) the death of another person, if the employer does not accept it` THEN 0
    WHEN `(c) the end of the employee's pregnancy by miscarriage or still-birth` THEN 3
    WHEN `(d) the end of another person's pregnancy by miscarriage or still-birth, for a spouse, partner, intended parent or primary carer` THEN 3
    WHEN `(d) the end of another person's pregnancy, for anyone else` THEN 0

-- s 70(2) "If an employee suffers more than 1 bereavement at the same time,
-- he or she may take the amount of bereavement leave specified in
-- subsection (1) in respect of each bereavement."
@ref s 70
GIVEN bs IS A LIST OF Bereavement
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the bereavement leave for all of` bs MEANS
    sum (map `the bereavement leave for` bs)

§§ `Family violence leave (ss 72B, 72C, 72H)`

-- s 72B(1) "a person affected by family violence means a person who is 1 or
-- both of the following: (a) a person against whom any other person is
-- inflicting, or has inflicted, family violence: (b) a person with whom
-- there ordinarily or periodically resides a child against whom any other
-- person is inflicting, or has inflicted, family violence."
@ref s 72B
GIVEN f IS A `Family violence facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a person affected by family violence` f IF
        f's `family violence is inflicted, or has been inflicted, on the person`
    OR  f's `a child against whom family violence is or was inflicted resides with the person`

-- The day Part 2 of the Domestic Violence—Victims' Protection Act 2018
-- inserted subpart 5 (1 April 2019; the date is that Act's, not this
-- text's). Schedule 1AA cl 4 reaches family violence that occurred before
-- it, but leave cannot be taken under a subpart not yet in force.
`the 2019 commencement date` MEANS Date 1 4 2019

§§ `The leave position (ss 63-72J)`

DECLARE `Leave position` HAS
    `entitled to the leave`                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `days of leave allowed`                             IS A NUMBER
    `days deducted from the entitlement`                IS A NUMBER
    `leave pay`                                         IS A NUMBER
    `when leave pay is due`                             IS A `When payment is due`

-- s 63(3), s 72D(3) "an employer and employee may agree that— (a) the
-- employee may take sick leave [or family violence leave] in advance; and
-- (b) ... the amount of leave taken is to be deducted from the employee's
-- entitlement".
@ref ss 63, 65, 69, 72B, 72C, 72D
GIVEN l IS A Leave
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `entitled to take the leave` l IF
        (`entitled under s 63(1) or 72D(1)` (l's `service`) `on` (l's `leave begins on`) OR l's `agreed to take leave in advance`)
    AND CONSIDER l's `kind of leave`
        WHEN `sick leave`            THEN `a ground for sick leave` (l's `whose sickness or injury`)
        WHEN `bereavement leave`     THEN `the bereavement leave for all of` (l's `bereavements`) GREATER THAN 0
        WHEN `family violence leave` THEN `a person affected by family violence` (l's `family violence`)

-- s 65(2) the days of sick leave; s 70 the days of bereavement leave; s 72H
-- "(a) may take up to 10 days' family violence leave in each of the 12-month
-- periods". The days left in the current entitlement are an input.
@ref ss 65, 70, 72H
GIVEN l IS A Leave
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days of leave allowed for` l MEANS
    IF   `entitled to take the leave` l
    THEN CONSIDER l's `kind of leave`
         WHEN `sick leave`            THEN min asked (max 0 (l's `days left in the current entitlement`))
         WHEN `bereavement leave`     THEN min asked (`the bereavement leave for all of` (l's `bereavements`))
         WHEN `family violence leave` THEN min asked (min 10 (max 0 (l's `days left in the current entitlement`)))
    ELSE 0
    WHERE
        asked MEANS l's `days of leave on otherwise working days`

-- s 71(1) "An employer must pay an employee an amount that is equivalent to
-- the employee's relevant daily pay or average daily pay for each day of
-- sick leave or bereavement leave taken by the employee that would otherwise
-- be a working day for the employee. (2) Despite subsection (1), an employer
-- is not required to pay an employee for any time for which the employee is
-- paid weekly compensation". s 72I(1)-(2) in the same words for family
-- violence leave. s 72 "(1) ... in the pay that relates to the pay period in
-- which the leave is taken. (2) However, if an employee is required to
-- provide proof ... and fails, without reasonable excuse, to do so, the
-- employer is not required to pay ... until the employee complies". s 72J
-- the same.
@ref ss 63-72, 72A-72J; Schedule 1AA cls 4, 5
@export The position for a spell of sick, bereavement or family violence leave under ss 63-72J of the Holidays Act 2003 (NZ): whether the employee is entitled (6 months' current continuous employment, or the hours test, or an agreement to take leave in advance; and a ground for the leave), how many days are allowed (sick and family violence leave up to the balance, family violence up to 10; bereavement 3 days for a close relative or pregnancy loss and 1 day for another death the employer accepts, for each bereavement), the leave pay (the daily pay for each day, less days paid ACC weekly compensation), and when it is due. DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN l IS A Leave  @desc The leave: its kind and first day, the employee's service, the days asked on otherwise working days, the days left in the current entitlement, any agreement to take it in advance, the ground (whose sickness, the bereavements, the family violence facts), the daily pay facts, days paid ACC weekly compensation, and whether required proof was not produced without reasonable excuse
GIVETH A `Leave position`
`the leave position for` l MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF fvTooEarly   THEN `family violence leave before 1 April 2019 was not available: subpart 5 was not yet in force`
        IF sickTooEarly THEN `sick leave in a 12-month period that began before 24 July 2021 falls under s 65(2) as it stood before the 2021 amendment, which this encoding does not carry`
        OTHERWISE `Leave position` WITH
            `entitled to the leave`                 IS `entitled to take the leave` l
            `days of leave allowed`                 IS allowed
            `days deducted from the entitlement`    IS IF l's `kind of leave` EQUALS `bereavement leave` THEN 0 ELSE allowed
            `leave pay`                             IS paid
            `when leave pay is due`                 IS due
    WHERE
        starts  MEANS l's `leave begins on`
        fvTooEarly MEANS
                l's `kind of leave` EQUALS `family violence leave`
            AND starts LESS THAN `the 2019 commencement date`
        sickTooEarly MEANS
                l's `kind of leave` EQUALS `sick leave`
            AND `entitled under s 63(1) or 72D(1)` (l's `service`) `on` starts
            AND `the first day of the 12-month entitlement period containing` starts `for` (l's `service`) LESS THAN `the 2021 commencement date`
        allowed MEANS `the days of leave allowed for` l
        due     MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF paid EQUALS 0 THEN `nothing is payable`
                IF l's `proof was required and not produced without reasonable excuse` THEN `when the employee produces the proof required`
                OTHERWISE `in the pay for the pay period in which the holiday or leave falls`
        paid    MEANS max 0 (allowed MINUS l's `days for which ACC weekly compensation is paid`) TIMES `the daily pay` (l's `daily pay for the leave`)

`sick leave in a 12-month period that began before 24 July 2021 falls under s 65(2) as it stood before the 2021 amendment, which this encoding does not carry` MEANS
    REFUSE "sick leave in a 12-month period that began before 24 July 2021 falls under s 65(2) as it stood before the 2021 amendment, which this encoding does not carry"

`family violence leave before 1 April 2019 was not available: subpart 5 was not yet in force` MEANS
    REFUSE "family violence leave before 1 April 2019 was not available: subpart 5 was not yet in force"

-- s 71(4) "if an employer pays the difference between the employee's first
-- week compensation or weekly compensation and ordinary weekly pay, the
-- employer may agree with the employee that he or she may deduct from the
-- employee's current sick leave entitlement 1 day for every 5 whole days
-- that the employer makes that payment." s 72I(4) the same for family
-- violence leave.
@ref ss 71(4), 72I(4)
GIVEN days IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days deducted for topping up ACC compensation, whole days topped up` days MEANS
    FLOOR (days DIVIDED BY 5)

-- s 67 "An employee is not entitled to be paid for any sick leave that has
-- not been taken before the date on which his or her employment ends." s 72F
-- the same for family violence leave. s 72H(b) family violence leave
-- "cannot carry forward".
@ref ss 67, 72F
`the pay for untaken sick or family violence leave at the end of employment` MEANS 0
